"Do what thou wilt shall be the whole of the law."

This is the foundational motto of the fringe occultist practice called Thelema.
The interpretations of this define a very important distinction, that when applied to greater religion, let us identify the layman from the mystic.

## The Value of the Mystic
Evelyn Underhill has a wonderful observation about the "mystical
spirit." She claims that while the layman pras for extrinsic
reasons. He prays to adhere to tradition, or when he is in need. While
the mystic prays for the sake of praying. Living in a godly way is not
for the purpose of obtaining a favorable afterlife, but simply becuase
it is the way to be.

## ...is to will one thing.
The ultimate distinction between these two are in the word "wilt." A
common definition of will is synonomous with desire. Both are "want."
Kierkegaard has a really good essay called Purity of Heart. Only past
the introduction does he give away the full title, "Purity of Heart
... is to will one thing." That thing being good. This puts will at an
interesting place. It's not /just/ about desire, it’s about fufilling yourself. This definition of will, the quote now means something different. "It is the greatest possible freedom and the strictest possible promise." 
